+## A whole run, start to finish (Linux, bash, from the repository root)
+
+This is the run the checks were written for: the desk sends its mail to the catcher, the load driver sends made-up leads, and the checks read what the desk, the catcher and the demo CRM hold afterwards. It was run once as written, on a copy of this tree, on one Linux computer with no network except loopback: the driver sent 30 requests (24 different request numbers, because 6 were repeats), the desk made 21 leads and sent 21 emails to the catcher, and all seven checks held (exit code 0). The log of that run is not shipped, and no test repeats it, so a later change to a tool can make this recipe out of date without a red test.
+
+The recipe is safe to start next to the "Try it" demo of the main README: it uses its own ports (desk 18765, demo CRM 18766, catcher 12525) and its own desk book `data/load/desk.sqlite3`, and its first line deletes the folder `data/load` (git-ignored, inside the repository, which is where the tools accept paths) so that no earlier book or log is mixed in. Each server is waited for (up to 20 seconds) before the next step; if a line prints `no answer from <address>`, stop the programs with `kill $(jobs -p)` and read the logs in `data/load`. The run takes a few seconds.
+
+    export PYTHONPATH=src:.
+    rm -rf data/load && mkdir -p data/load                    # a fresh folder, so no earlier book or log is mixed in
+    wait_for() { for i in $(seq 1 20); do curl -sf "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0; sleep 1; done; echo "no answer from $1" >&2; return 1; }
+    python load_tools/catcher.py --out data/load/mail.jsonl --port 12525 > data/load/catcher.log 2>&1 &
+    python -m demo_only.fakes_server --outbox keyed --port 18766 > data/load/fakes.log 2>&1 &
+    wait_for http://127.0.0.1:18766/fake/crm
+    DESK_DB_PATH=data/load/desk.sqlite3 DESK_PORT=18765 DESK_FAKES_URL=http://127.0.0.1:18766 DESK_OUTBOX=smtp DESK_SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1 DESK_SMTP_PORT=12525 python -m lead_desk.server > data/load/desk.log 2>&1 &
+    wait_for http://127.0.0.1:18765/counts
+    python -m load_tools.load_driver --base-url http://127.0.0.1:18765 --scenario dupmix:30:0.3 --log data/load/req.jsonl
+    for i in $(seq 1 60); do curl -s http://127.0.0.1:18765/counts | grep -q '"pending_followups": 0' && break; sleep 1; done; curl -s http://127.0.0.1:18765/counts
+    curl -s http://127.0.0.1:18766/fake/crm > data/load/crm.json
+    : > data/load/model.jsonl
+    python -m load_tools.check_invariants --requests data/load/req.jsonl --book data/load/desk.sqlite3 --catcher data/load/mail.jsonl --crm data/load/crm.json --model-log data/load/model.jsonl
+    echo "check_invariants exit code: $?"
+    kill $(jobs -p)
+
+Why each part is there: `DESK_OUTBOX=smtp` makes the desk send real mail messages (to the catcher on this computer) instead of calling the demo mail stand-in, so the catcher log can show what arrived; the demo CRM stand-in (started by `demo_only.fakes_server`) still receives the CRM calls, and `/fake/crm` is its dump for check 4. The empty `model.jsonl` is right for this run: the desk uses the pre-written model answers, so there is no model call to record and check 5 reports 0 calls for 0 leads (to see real call counts, start the fake model server described below with `--call-log` and point the desk at it). The checks print a table and then a JSON result; the exit code is 0 only when all seven hold. The last line stops the three background programs. To run again against the same desk book, give the driver a new `--id-prefix` (see Scenarios below); the recipe above starts from a fresh folder each time.
+
 ## Input and output formats (the contract between the tools)
 
 All times are epoch seconds as floating point numbers (`time.time()` on the same machine). One JSON object per line
